People affected by the wildfires in Los Angeles should have a say in planning its recovery. So the State of California brought them together with their government in a new way.

With Engaged California, state and local officials listened to and learned from survivors. This helps officials co-create recovery policy with the community.

----
id: what_we_heard
----

## What we heard

We worked with over 900 people affected by the Eaton and Palisades wildfires. Their input helps shape long-term recovery plans.

----
id: overview
----

## Overview

Our team worked with community members in greater Los Angeles. We focused on Altadena and Pacific Palisades. Together, we navigated complex conversations and differing views on fire recovery. We identified the issues that are the most important to you.


----
id: about
----

### About the process

Engaged California is an example of deliberative democracy. It empowers you to engage with your government in a new way. It offers a space for meaningful dialogue. This builds understanding and shapes policy—before decisions are made.

[Learn more about Engaged California](/about)


